 "result": "**Changed:** the request now identifies the channel by \"our workspace owner\" instead of \"our workspace admin.\" The reader was right that \"admin\" is ambiguous — a colleague could loosely call the owner \"an admin,\" which would let `C_OWNER` (renamed `C_ADMIN`) also qualify, breaking uniqueness. \"Owner\" doesn't have that problem: it's the singular top role, and no one would loosely call an admin \"the owner\" (the conflation only runs one direction). I swapped Victor to owner (now the anchor for the target) and Amara to admin (now the adjacent-role decoy, renamed `C_ADMIN`), updated the reference query's identity filter to `U_VICTOR`, and updated all decoy witnesses/explanations accordingly. The member-count and date decoys are otherwise unchanged, just re-pointed at the owner instead of the admin.",
